n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n-k-1,\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n  (there are \\(n-k-1\\) of them; when \\(k=n-1\\) this list is empty);\n\n* the horizontal line  \n\n  \\[\n  y=1 .\n  \\tag{3}\n  \\]\n\nAll these lines are distinct and none of them is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence they are non‑sunny.\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k-1\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nEvery point with \\(b=1\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nThus the points that are **not** yet covered are precisely\n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge n-k,\\;b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA short count shows that  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\sum_{a=n-k}^{n}(n+1-a-1)=\\sum_{t=0}^{k}t=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nGeometrically, \\(T_k\\) is the right‑triangle with vertices \\((n-k,2),\\;(n,2),\\;(n-k,k+1)\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The sunny part  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le k-1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y-2 = (x-(n-k+i)),\n\\qquad\\text{ i.e. }\\; y = x-(n-k+i)+2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe points of \\(L_i\\) that belong to \\(S_n\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(n-k+i+j,\\;2+j)\\mid j=0,1,\\dots ,k-i-1\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the set of points of \\(L_i\\) that lie in \\(T_k\\) is a diagonal of slope \\(1\\) running from the bottom side of the triangle to its hypotenuse.  \n\nWhen \\(i\\) runs from \\(0\\) to \\(k-1\\) these diagonals are disjoint and their union is precisely \\(T_k\\).  \nHence every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\).\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) together with the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (2)–(3) cover **all** points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+(k)=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nTherefore for the chosen \\(k\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* By the argument of §1, any admissible configuration must contain at least one non‑sunny line, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \n* By the construction of §2, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n-1\\,\\}} .\n\\]"
