me \\(x\\)-coordinate modulo the denominator of \\(m_i\\), which is impossible for the chosen points).\n\n   *Final family.*  \n   Remove from the diagonal family the \\(k\\) diagonals \\(D_{i+1}\\) that contain the points \\(P_i\\) and keep the remaining \\(n-k\\) diagonals.  The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{D_s\\mid s\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on a diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \n   If \\(s\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) the diagonal is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and the point is covered.  \n   If \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) then the point is precisely the chosen \\(P_{s-1}\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_{s-1}\\).  Hence all required points are covered.\n\n   Therefore a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper‑bound argument shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible for all even \\(n\\) and for all odd \\(n\\ge5\\); an explicit example shows that \\(k=n\\) is possible when \\(n=3\\).  The construction above shows that every smaller value of \\(k\\) can be realised.  Consequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{for all }n\\ge3,\\quad\\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3.}\n   \\]"
