\\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) odd‑sum points, a line of this type can contain at most \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points **only** when it uses the even diagonals; a second line of the same type would have to use the odd diagonals, but there are not enough odd‑sum points to reach \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\).  Hence a partition of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) into \\(n\\) such lines is impossible.\n\n   *Case \\(|p+q|=2\\).*  \n   Here the diagonal indices of the line have the same parity as the starting diagonal, and the step size is \\(2\\).  Thus each line can meet at most \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) diagonals, but **exactly one** of the two possible parities (even or odd).  As before, there are only \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) even‑sum points and \\(\\frac{n-1}{2}\\) odd‑sum points, so at most one line can achieve the required \\(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points; the remaining \\(n-1\\) lines would fall short.  \n\n   In both sub‑cases we obtain a contradiction, therefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) cannot exist for any odd \\(n\\ge5\\).\n\n   *The case \\(n=3\\).*  \n   Here \\(|\\mathcal P_3|=3\\) and \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=2\\).  The bound (1) does not force a contradiction, and an explicit construction (three distinct sunny lines each passing through one of the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\)) shows that \\(k=n=3\\) is attainable.\n\n   Summarising, a full‑sunny configuration exists **only** for \\(n=3\\); for every other \\(n\\ge3\\) we must have \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n3. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\le n-1\\).**  \n\n   *Base covering.*  \n   For \\(s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_s:\\;x+y=s .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(D_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore **not** sunny.  The family \\(\\{D_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\) covers all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=s\\) for a unique \\(s\\)).\n\n   *Replacing some diagonals by sunny lines.*  \n   Fix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \n   Choose the points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,1)\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The point \\(P_i\\) lies on the diagonal \\(D_{i+1}\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\) pick a rational slope \\(m_i\\) such that  \n\n   \\[\n   m_i\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\\qquad m_i\\neq m_j\\;(i\\neq j)\n   \\]\n\n   (for instance \\(m_i=i\\)).  Define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   S_i:\\;y-1=m_i(x-i).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because the slopes are distinct and none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), each \\(S_i\\) is sunny, passes through \\(P_i\\), and contains **no other** point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (any other lattice point on \\(S_i\\) would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate modulo the denominator of \\(m_i\\), which is impossible for the chosen points).\n\n   *Final family.*  \n   Remove from the diagonal family the \\(k\\) diagonals \\(D_{i+1}\\) that contain the points \\(P_i\\) and keep the remaining \\(n-k\\) diagonals.  The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\}\\cup\\{D_s\\mid s\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n   Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on a diagonal \\(D_s\\).  \n   If \\(s\\notin\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) the diagonal is still present in \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) and the point is covered.  \n   If \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) then the point is precisely the chosen \\(P_{s-1}\\) and lies on the sunny line \\(S_{s-1}\\).  Hence all required points are covered.\n\n   Therefore a configuration exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The upper‑bound argument shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible for all even \\(n\\) and for all odd \\(n\\ge5\\); an explicit example shows that \\(k=n\\) is possible when \\(n=3\\).  The construction above shows that every smaller value of \\(k\\) can be realised.  Consequently the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\ \\text{for all }n\\ge3,\\quad\\text{and additionally }k=n\\text{ when }n=3.}\n   \\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
