            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  At least one non‑sunny line is necessary  \n\nThe column  \n\n\\[\nC_1=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset S_n\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) points.  \nA sunny line is not vertical, hence it meets \\(C_1\\) in at most one point.  \nWith only \\(n\\) lines available, if all of them were sunny we could cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(C_1\\); the remaining points of \\(C_1\\) would stay uncovered.  \nThus any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines must contain at least one non‑sunny line, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A stronger restriction on sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line and let  \n\n\\[\nC_a=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}\\qquad(a\\ge1)\n\\]\n\nbe the \\(a\\)-th column of \\(S_n\\).  \nThen \\(\\ell\\) contains **at most one** point of \\(C_a\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(\\ell\\) contained two distinct points of the same column, those two points would have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, forcing \\(\\ell\\) to be vertical, contradicting the definition of sunny. ∎\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nAll sunny lines together can contain at most \\(2k\\) points of the union \\(C_1\\cup C_2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy Lemma 1 a sunny line contributes at most one point of column 1 and at most one point of column 2; it cannot contribute more than two points in total from the two leftmost columns. Summing over the \\(k\\) sunny lines yields the bound \\(2k\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Counting argument forcing \\(k=0\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_n\\) contains  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\npoints (the triangular number).  \nColumns 1 and 2 together contain  \n\n\\[\n|C_1|+|C_2|=n+(n-1)=2n-1\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\npoints.\n\nAssume that a configuration with exactly \\(k\\ge1\\) sunny lines exists.  \nBy Lemma 2 the sunny lines can cover at most \\(2k\\) points of the set (4).  \nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\n(2n-1)-2k\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\npoints of the first two columns must be covered by the non‑sunny lines.\n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\).  \n* A vertical non‑sunny line can contain **at most one** point of column 2 (otherwise it would be vertical, which is allowed, but then it would also contain all points of column 1 lying on that same line; however column 1 already contains a point on each vertical line, so the contribution to (5) from a vertical line is at most one new point of column 2).  \n* A horizontal line contains no point of column 2.  \n* A line of slope \\(-1\\) meets column 2 in at most one point (otherwise two points of column 2 would have the same sum of coordinates, forcing slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\nHence **each non‑sunny line can contribute at most one new point** to the set (5).  \nSince there are only \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\;\\ge\\;(2n-1)-2k .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 0 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\) is a non‑negative integer, the only possibility is  \n\n\\[\nk=0 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration with a sunny line can satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for \\(k=0\\)\n\nDefine the three non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=1,\\qquad\n\\ell_3:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n* \\(\\ell_1\\) contains every point of column 1.  \n* \\(\\ell_2\\) contains every point of row 1.  \n* \\(\\ell_3\\) contains every point of \\(S_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\) (the “upper” side of the triangle).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_1\\).  \n* If \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_2\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\).  \n  The inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a+b=n+1\\); otherwise \\(a+b\\le n\\), which is impossible for integers \\(a,b\\ge2\\).  \n  Hence \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_3\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of the three lines (7).  \nAdding any \\(n-3\\) further distinct non‑sunny lines (for instance the vertical lines \\(x=2,3,\\dots ,n-2\\) when \\(n\\ge4\\)) yields a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, all of which are non‑sunny, that satisfies the required covering condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe counting argument of Section 3 shows that a sunny line cannot appear in any admissible family; consequently the only feasible value of \\(k\\) is \\(0\\).  \nSection 4 provides an explicit construction for \\(k=0\\) for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nHence the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which the required configuration exists is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
