e original set \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate exceeds \\(k\\).  \n\n   Because the points with first coordinate \\(\\le k\\) are already covered by the sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\), the whole family of \\(n\\) lines (the \\(k\\) sunny ones and the \\(n-k\\) vertical ones) satisfies the required covering condition.  \n\n   The construction yields exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, proving that every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is feasible.\n\n3. **Verification of distinctness.**  \n   The vertical lines \\(v_c\\) are pairwise distinct.  \n   The sunny lines supplied by Lemma\u202f1 are distinct by construction, and none of them is vertical, horizontal, or of slope \\(-1\\); hence they are distinct from the vertical lines.  \n\nThus the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Sunny\u2011covering of a triangular lattice)\n\nFor every integer \\(m\\ge3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(S_m\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite the points of \\(S_m\\) as a triangular array with rows indexed by the second coordinate \\(b\\) and columns by the first coordinate \\(a\\).  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\; y = \\frac{t}{t+1}\\,x + \\frac{1}{t+1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_t\\) passes through the two lattice points  \n\n\\[\nP_t=(t,1)\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad Q_t=(1,t+1),\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nboth of which belong to \\(S_m\\) because \\(t\\le m\\) and \\(t+1\\le m+1\\).  \nThe slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{t}{t+1}\\); this number is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for any positive integer \\(t\\), so each \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.  \n\nWe claim that the \\(m\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\) together contain every point of \\(S_m\\).  \nIndeed, let \\((a,b)\\in S_m\\). Because \\(a+b\\le m+1\\), we have \\(b\\le m+1-a\\). Set  \n\n\\[\nt:=a-1\\quad(\\text{so }0\\le t\\le m-1).\n\\]\n\nThen \\(b\\le m+1-a = m-t\\). If \\(b=m-t\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) coincides with \\(Q_{t}\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_{t}\\).  \nIf \\(b<m-t\\) we write \\(b = m-t-s\\) with a positive integer \\(s\\). Solving the equation  \n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{t}{t+1}a + \\frac{1}{t+1}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nfor \\(a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na = \\frac{(t+1)b-1}{t}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\le m-t-1\\) and \\(t\\ge1\\), the right\u2013hand side of (5) is an integer equal to \\(a\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (4) and therefore lies on \\(\\ell_t\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_m\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\). The lines are distinct because they have distinct slopes \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary number of sunny lines\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Case\u202f\\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1, x=2,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \nAll of them are non\u2011sunny and together they contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Case\u202f\\(k\\ge1\\).*  \n\n1. **Sunny part.**  \n   If \\(k\\ge3\\) apply Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=k\\) and obtain the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) that cover the whole set \\(S_k\\).  \n   For the remaining values \\(k=1,2\\) we give explicit sunny lines:\n   * \\(k=1\\): the line \\(y=x\\) (slope\u202f1) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); together with the two vertical lines \\(x=2\\) and \\(x=3\\) (when \\(n\\ge3\\)) all points are covered.\n   * \\(k=2\\): the two