elongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\).  \nWe claim that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nIndeed, using the definition of \\(\\ell_i\\) and the value of \\(i\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nb\n&= -\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,a+\\frac{i+2}{i+1}\n   = -\\frac{m+1-a}{m+2-a}\\,a+\\frac{m+3-a}{m+2-a}  \\\\\n&= \\frac{-a(m+1-a)+(m+3-a)}{m+2-a}\n   = \\frac{-am-a+ a^{2}+m+3-a}{m+2-a}          \\\\\n&= \\frac{a^{2}-(a+1)m+ (m+2)}{m+2-a}\n   = \\frac{(a-1)(m+2-a)}{m+2-a}=a-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((a,b)\\in S_m\\) we have \\(b=m+2-a-a = m+2-2a\\); the above computation shows that this value coincides with the right–hand side of (2) for the chosen \\(i\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) and therefore belongs to \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_m\\) lies on one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\).  \nThe slopes \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) are pairwise different, so the lines are distinct. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary number of sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Sunny lines\n\n*If \\(k\\ge 3\\).*  \nApply Lemma 1 with \\(m=k\\).  \nWe obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) that together contain every point of \\(S_k\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\).*  \nNo sunny line is used.\n\n*If \\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the line \\(\\ell_1\\) from (2) with \\(i=1\\):\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\]\nIt passes through the points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\); consequently it contains the whole column \\(a=1\\), i.e. all points of \\(S_1\\).\n\n*If \\(k=2\\).*  \nTake \\(\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\) from (2) with \\(i=1,2\\):\n\\[\n\\ell_1:\\;y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2},\\qquad\n\\ell_2:\\;y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}.\n\\]\nA direct check shows that every point of \\(S_2\\) (the points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\)) lies on one of these two lines.\n\nIn every case the chosen sunny lines cover **all** points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Vertical (non‑sunny) lines\n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(k+1\\le c\\le n\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nv_c:\\;x=c .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny (they have infinite slope) and they are pairwise distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le k\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\); by the property of the sunny lines proved in Step 1, \\((a,b)\\) lies on one of \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\ge k+1\\)*, then the vertical line \\(v_a\\) (which exists because \\(a\\le n\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus each point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the constructed lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Count of sunny lines and distinctness\n\nExactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are sunny; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines \\(v_{k+1},\\dots ,v_n\\) are not.  \nAll lines are distinct: the vertical lines have slope \\(\\infty\\), whereas each \\(\\ell_i\\) has finite slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\neq\\infty\\); moreover the slopes \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) are pairwise different, so the \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct among themselves.\n\nConsequently we have produced exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and which together cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Exhaustiveness\n\nSince we have exhibited a construction for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are admissible.  \nConversely, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines, which is \\(n\\); therefore no integer \\(k>n\\) can occ