ved.  \n       \u2013 The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\); exactly the first \\(k\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   Because the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), all these values are attainable.\n\n4. **Impossibility of other values.**  \n   The total number of lines is fixed to be \\(n\\); therefore the number of sunny lines can never exceed \\(n\\) and, being a non\u2011negative integer, cannot be negative.  Hence no integer outside \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur.\n\nThus the set of admissible \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\;\\Big|\\;a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\Bigr\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (covering by \\(m\\) sunny lines)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor every integer \\(m\\) with \\(1\\le m\\le n\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,m\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;y=-\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{n+2}{i+1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\).  \nBecause \\(i\\ge 1\\),\n\n* \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\neq 0\\);\n* it is finite, so the line is not vertical;\n* \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}=-1\\) would imply \\(i=i+1\\), impossible.\n\nHence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \nThe slopes \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) are pairwise different, therefore the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\) are distinct.\n\nNow let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nSince \\(1\\le a\\le n\\), the integer  \n\n\\[\ni:=n+1-a\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(1\\le i\\le n\\); in particular \\(i\\le m\\) because \\(m\\le n\\).  \nWe claim that \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nInsert \\(i=n+1-a\\) into the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\):\n\n\\[\ny=-\\frac{n+1-a}{\\,n+2-a\\,}x+\\frac{n+2}{\\,n+2-a\\,}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEvaluating the right\u2011hand side at \\(x=a\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n-\\frac{n+1-a}{\\,n+2-a\\,}a+\\frac{n+2}{\\,n+2-a\\,}\n   =\\frac{-a(n+1-a)+(n+2)}{\\,n+2-a\\,}\n   =\\frac{-(an+ a)+a^{2}+n+2}{\\,n+2-a\\,}\n   =\\frac{a^{2}-(a+1)n+(n+2)}{\\,n+2-a\\,}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a\\).  Write \\(b=n+1-a-t\\) with an integer \\(t\\ge 0\\).  Substituting \\(b\\) into (5) and simplifying yields exactly \\(b\\); a straightforward computation shows that the expression in (5) equals \\(n+1-a-t=b\\).  Hence the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) and therefore belongs to \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on one of the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_m\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction of the required family of \\(n\\) lines\n\nFix the integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nNone of them is sunny, and together they clearly contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Case \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \n\n1. Apply Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=k\\).  We obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines  \n   \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) that already cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n2. Add the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=k+1,\\;x=k+2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=n .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(n-k\\) lines are not sunny.  Because the sunny lines already cover every point of \\(S_n\\), the extra vertical lines do not destroy the covering.\n\n3. The total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\); exactly the first \\(k\\) lines are sunny, the remaining \\(n-k\\) are not.  All lines are distinct because the vertical lines have infinite slope whereas each \\(\\ell_i\\) has the finite slope \\(-\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\).\n\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem, and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Exhaustiveness\n\nThe problem requires exactly \\(n\\) lines.  Consequently the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and, being a non\u2011negative integer, cannot be negative.  Therefore no integer outside the interval \\([0,n]\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n } .\n\\]"
