iguration with no sunny line  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThe set of points of \\(S_n\\) having abscissa \\(i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\},\n\\]\n\nhence every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\).  \nAll these lines are vertical, therefore none of them is sunny.  \nConsequently a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive step: from \\(t\\) sunny lines to \\(t+1\\) sunny lines  \n\nAssume that for some \\(t\\;(0\\le t<n)\\) we already have a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_t=\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_t\\}\\cup\\{V_{t+1},\\dots ,V_n\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, where  \n\n* the \\(M_i\\) are sunny,\n* the \\(V_j\\) are vertical (hence non‑sunny).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nc:=\\min\\{\\,j\\mid V_j\\text{ is present in }\\mathcal F_t\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(V_c\\) is the left‑most vertical line in the family.\nBecause the points of column \\(c\\) are covered only by \\(V_c\\) (no sunny line can contain two points of the same column), the point  \n\n\\[\nP_c:=(c,\\,n+1-c)\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n(the highest point of that column) is covered **only** by \\(V_c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Replacing \\(V_c\\) by a sunny line  \n\nConsider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_c:\\;y=x-c+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(1\\); therefore \\(\\ell_c\\) is sunny and it passes through the two points  \n\n\\[\nQ_c:=(c,1),\\qquad Q_{c+1}:=(c+1,2).\n\\]\n\nBoth points belong to \\(S_n\\) because \\(c\\ge 1\\) and \\(c+1\\le n\\).\nConsequently \\(\\ell_c\\) covers the point \\(Q_{c+1}\\) that was previously covered\nby the vertical line \\(V_{c+1}\\).\n\nReplace \\(V_c\\) by \\(\\ell_c\\) and delete the point \\(Q_{c+1}\\) from the\ncoverage of \\(V_{c+1}\\).  \nAll other points of column