c\\) remain covered by \\(\\ell_c\\) (they lie on the same line because they have the same abscissa \\(c\\) and the same ordinate as \\(Q_c\\) after a translation of slope \\(1\\)).  \nThus after this replacement the only uncovered point is \\(Q_{c+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Shifting the uncovered point to the right  \n\nIf \\(c+1=n\\) we stop: the uncovered point \\(Q_n=(n,1)\\) lies on the sunny line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_n:\\;y=x-n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is already present (it is \\(\\ell_c\\) with \\(c=n\\)).  \nHence the family now contains \\(t+1\\) sunny lines and still exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\nIf \\(c+1<n\\) we repeat the same operation with the vertical line\n\\(V_{c+1}\\): replace \\(V_{c+1}\\) by the sunny line \\(\\ell_{c+1}\\) of slope \\(1\\)\nthrough \\((c+1,1)\\) and \\((c+2,2)\\).  \nThis new sunny line covers the point \\((c+2,2)\\) and leaves the point\n\\((c+2,1)\\) uncovered.  \n\nThus each step moves the unique uncovered point one column to the right,\nwhile increasing the number of sunny lines by one.\nBecause there are only finitely many columns, after at most\n\\(n-c\\) steps the uncovered point reaches column \\(n\\), where it is\nalready covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell_n\\).\n\nConsequently we have transformed the original family \\(\\mathcal F_t\\) into a\nnew family \\(\\mathcal F_{t+1}\\) that\n\n* still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n* covers every point of \\(S_n\\),\n* contains exactly \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the induction  \n\nStarting from the configuration of Section 1 (which has \\(t=0\\) sunny\nlines) and applying the inductive step repeatedly, we obtain configurations\nwith \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) sunny lines.\nHence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of other values  \n\nThe definition of a sunny line is binary; a line is either sunny or not.\nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines can never be\nnegative nor exceed \\(n\\).  Therefore the only possible values are the\nintegers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), all of which have been shown to be\nrealizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\{\\,k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\mid 0\\le k\\le n\\,\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) can occur, and no other\ninteger can."
