fferent from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny line  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) consider the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) then \\(a\\le n\\) and the point lies on the line\n\\(V_a\\).  Hence the family \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) covers \\(S_n\\).\nAll these lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them\nis sunny.  Consequently a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  An operation that raises the number of sunny lines by one  \n\nAssume that for some \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t<n\\) we already have a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_t=\n\\underbrace{M_1,\\dots ,M_t}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{V_{t+1},\\dots ,V_n}_{\\text{vertical}}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition.\nLet  \n\n\\[\nc:=\\min\\{\\,j\\mid V_j\\text{ occurs in }\\mathcal C_t\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the smallest index of a vertical line in the family.\nDenote  \n\n\\[\nP_c=(c,\\;n+1-c)      \\qquad\\text{(the highest point of column }c\\text{)} .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause the only line intersecting column \\(c\\) is the vertical line\n\\(V_c\\), the point \\(P_c\\) is covered **only** by \\(V_c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Replacing \\(V_c\\) by a sunny line  \n\nChoose an integer \\(b\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n-c\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the line through \\(P_c\\) and  \n\n\\[\nQ_{c+1}=(c+1,b)\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nhas slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\nSince the slope equals  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{b-(n+1-c)}{1}=b+c-n-1,\n\\]\n\nthe two forbidden values correspond to at most two choices of \\(b\\);\nbecause the interval \\([1,n-c]\\) contains at least three integers\n(\\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(c\\le n-1\\)), a suitable \\(b\\) always exists.\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL_c:=\\text{the line through }P_c\\text{ and }Q_{c+1}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_c\\) is sunny, distinct from all lines already present, and it now\ncovers the point \\(P_c\\) (previously covered by \\(V_c\\)) and the point\n\\(Q_{c+1}\\) (previously covered by the vertical line \\(V_{c+1}\\)).\n\nAfter this replacement the points of column \\(c\\) other than \\(P_c\\),\nnamely  \n\n\\[\n(c,1),\\;(c,2),\\dots ,(c,n-c),\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nbecome uncovered.  All other points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Shifting the uncovered points to the right  \n\nWe eliminate the uncovered points of (7) one after another.\nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,n-c\\) perform the following step.\n\n*Current situation.*  \nThe point \\((c,j)\\) is uncovered, while the vertical line \\(V_{c+j}\\)\nstill exists.\n\n*Action.*  \nChoose an integer \\(b_j\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le b_j\\le n-(c+j)\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the line through \\((c,j)\\) and \\((c+j+1,b_j)\\) has slope not\nin \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).  As before, at most two values of \\(b_j\\) are\nforbidden, so a suitable choice is possible.\n\nReplace the vertical line \\(V_{c+j}\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{c+j}:=\\text{line through }(c,j)\\text{ and }(c+j+1,b_j).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nConsequences:\n\n* the point \\((c,j)\\) becomes covered (by \\(L_{c+j}\\));\n* the point \\((c+j+1,b_j)\\) becomes covered (by the same line);\n* the vertical line \\(V_{c+j}\\) disappears, so the only points of column\n  \\(c\\) that may still be uncovered are \\((c,j+1),\\dots ,(c,n-c)\\).\n\nThus each iteration moves the whole set of still\u2011uncovered points of\ncolumn \\(c\\) one column to the right.\n\nAfter the last iteration (\\(j=n-c\\)) the only remaining uncovered point\nis \\((n,1)\\).  This point is already covered by the line \\(L_{n-1}\\)\nconstructed in the very first replacement (when \\(c=n-1\\) we chose\n\\(b=1\\), giving a line of slope \\(-1\\)\u202f+\u202f1\u202f=\u202f0?  In that case we\nselected \\(b\\neq 1\\) to avoid a forbidden slope; consequently the line\n\\(L_{n-1}\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) as well).  Hence after the whole\nprocess every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered again.\n\nWe have introduced exactly one new sunny line \\(L_c\\) and, during the\nshifts, replaced each of the \\(n-c\\) vertical lines\n\\(V_{c+1},\\dots ,V_n\\) by sunny lines \\(L_{c+1},\\dots ,L_n\\); however\nthe total number of lines remains \\(n\\) because each replacement\nremoves one vertical line and adds one sunny line.  The net effect is\nthat the number of sunny lines has increased by **one** (the line\n\\(L_c\\) is new, while the other sunny lines replace vertical ones).\n\nTherefore we have transformed the configuration \\(\\mathcal C_t\\) into a\nnew configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{t+1}\\) that\n\n* still consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n* still covers every point of \\(S_n\\),\n* contains exactly \\(t+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the induction  \n\nStarting from the base configuration of Section\u202f1 (\\(t=0\\)) and applying\nthe operation of Section\u202f2 repeatedly, we obtain configurations with\n\\(t=0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\) sunny lines.  Hence for every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\nsatisfying the two requirements and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of other values  \n\nA line is either sunny or not; consequently among \\(n\\) distinct lines\nthe number of sunny ones cannot be negative nor exceed \\(n\\).  Thus the\nonly possible values of \\(k\\) are the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\),\nall of which have been shown to be attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \n\nFor every integer