unny part\n\nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_i\\) is not sunny.  The set of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on at least\none of the \\(V_i\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m} (V_i\\cap T_n)\n   =\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a\\le m\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the points **not** covered by the vertical lines are  \n\n\\[\nS_{m}:=T_n\\setminus\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m} (V_i\\cap T_n)\n      =\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a>m\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nA direct count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_{m}|=\\sum_{a=m+1}^{n}(n+1-a)\n       =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The sunny part\n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nd\\in\\{m,m+1,\\dots ,m+k-1\\}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\ndefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\;y=x-d .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_d\\) equals \\(+1\\); therefore every \\(L_d\\) is sunny.\nThe values of \\(d\\) in (12) are distinct, so the lines \\(L_d\\) are distinct,\nand there are exactly \\(k\\) of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  The covering property\n\nWe claim that  \n\n\\[\nS_{m}= \\bigcup_{d=m}^{m+k-1}\\bigl(L_d\\cap T_n\\bigr).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_{m}\\).  Because \\(a>m\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nd:=a-b\\ge a-(n+1-a)=2a-n-1\\ge m .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nFrom the defining inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nd=a-b\\le n-1\\le m+k-1 .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(d\\) satisfies (12) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_d\\) given by\n(13).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the right–hand side of (14).\n\nConversely, if \\((a,b)\\in L_d\\cap T_n\\) for some \\(d\\) satisfying (12), then\n\\(a-b=d\\ge m\\); consequently \\(a>m\\) and \\((a,b)\\in S_{m}\\).  ∎\n\nBecause the sets \\(L_d\\cap T_n\\) are pairwise disjoint (different values of\n\\(d\\) give different values of \\(a-b\\)), the total number of points covered\nby the \\(k\\) sunny lines equal