ight\u2013hand side of (14), i.e. \\(|S_{m}|\\),\nwhich by (11) is exactly the number of points that were not covered by the\nvertical lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  The final family of lines\n\nCollect the \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines (8) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines (13).  The\nfamily consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; by (9) and (14) every point of\n\\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.  Moreover precisely the \\(k\\) lines\n\\(L_d\\) are sunny.  Hence we have produced a configuration that satisfies the\ntwo conditions of the problem and contains exactly the prescribed number \\(k\\)\nof sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Exhaustion of possibilities\n\nWe have shown that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable\nfamily of \\(n\\) lines exists.  Together with the obvious bound (6) this proves\nthat the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the answer to the problem is that all non\u2011negative integers not\nexceeding \\(n\\) can occur as the number of sunny lines.**"
