h \\((1,2)\\)) and \\(y=2x-3\\) (through \\((2,1)\\)).\n\n* \\(k=3\\): three sunny lines, each passing through a distinct point of \\(T_3\\); for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=2x-1,\\qquad y=3x-5 .\n  \\]\n\nAll four families consist of three distinct lines, satisfy the covering condition, and contain exactly the prescribed number of sunny lines. Hence the statement is true for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) the following **induction hypothesis** holds:\n\n> For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set  \n> \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k)\\) are sunny.\n\nWe shall construct, for the next integer \\(n+1\\), families \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k')\\) for all \\(k'\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k'\\le n\\)\n\nTake the configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\) supplied by the induction hypothesis.  \nAdd the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{v}: \\; x=n+1 .\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  The line \\(x=n+1\\) does not appear in \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\) because all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\) intersect the strip \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) (otherwise they could not cover any point of \\(T_n\\)).  \n\n*Sunny count.*  The added line is vertical, hence non‑sunny; therefore the number of sunny lines remains \\(k'\\).\n\n*Coverage of the new points.*  The set \\(T_{n+1}\\setminus T_n\\) consists of the single lattice point \\((n+1,1)\\) (the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+2\\) forces \\(b=1\\) when \\(a=n+1\\)).  This point lies on \\(L_{v}\\).  All points of \\(T_n\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n(k')\\).  \n\nConsequently \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(k'):=\\mathcal L_n(k')\\cup\\{L_{v}\\}\\) satisfies the required properties.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k'=n+1\\)\n\nStart from a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_n(n)\\) that has **all** its \\(n\\) lines sunny; such a configuration exists by the induction hypothesis (take \\(k=n\\)).  \nAdd the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_{s}: \\; y = x - n .\n\\]\n\n*Distinctness.*  Its slope is \\(1\\), which is different from the slopes of the \\(n\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_n(n)\\) (otherwise two distinct lines would be parallel, contradicting the fact that they already cover distinct points of \\(T_n\\)).  \n\n*Sunny count.*  The added line has slope \\(1\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.  The total number of sunny lines becomes \\(n+1\\).\n\n*Coverage of the new points.*  As before, the only point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) not belonging to \\(T_n\\) is \\((n+1,1)\\).  Substituting \\(x=n+1\\) into the equation of \\(L_{s}\\) gives \\(y=1\\); thus \\((n+1,1)\\) lies on \\(L_{s}\\).  All points of \\(T_n\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal L_n(n)\\).\n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1}(n+1):=\\mathcal L_n(n)\\cup\\{L_{s}\\}\\) fulfills the required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the induction\n\nThe constructions of Sections 2.1 and 2.2 show that, assuming the induction hypothesis for \\(n\\), the hypothesis also holds for \\(n+1\\).  \nSince the base case \\(n=3\\) has been verified, the statement is true for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nFor each integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), inclusive.  In other words,\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nand every such \\(k\\) can be realized by an explicit configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n\n### Verification Ta