\\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n  \\]\n\n  are non‑sunny.  For any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Thus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  We prove the existence of a configuration consisting solely of sunny lines (Lemma 1 below).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lemma 1 – a sunny configuration with \\(n\\) lines  \n\nDefine a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(1)=1,\\qquad \n\\sigma(i)=\n\\begin{cases}\ni+1 & (2\\le i\\le n-1),\\\\[2pt]\n2   & (i=n).\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nClearly \\(\\sigma\\) is a bijection, \\(\\sigma(1)=1\\), and for every \\(i\\ge 2\\)\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\sigma(i)\\neq 1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let \\(L_i\\) be the line through the two points  \n\n\\[\nP_i=(1,\\sigma(i)),\\qquad Q_i=(i,1).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2) the slope of \\(L_i\\) equals  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{1-\\sigma(i)}{i-1},\n\\]\n\nwhich is never \\(0\\) (the numerator is non‑zero), never \\(\\infty\\) (the denominator is non‑zero for \\(i\\ge 2\\); for \\(i=1\\) the line is the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is non‑sunny, but \\(\\sigma(1)=1\\) makes \\(P_1=Q_1\\); we simply take \\(L_1\\) to be the vertical line \\(x=1\\), which is **not** sunny.  To obtain a *sunny* line for \\(i=1\\) we replace \\(L_1\\) by the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); its slope is \\(1\\), which is allowed.  Hence all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\nThe lines are distinct because each \\(L_i\\) contains the unique point \\((i,1)\\) with abscissa \\(i\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Lemma 2 – the lines \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) cover \\(T_n\\)\n\n*First row.*  \nFor any \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have \\(\\sigma^{-1}(b)\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  By construction the point \\((1,b)=P_{\\sigma^{-1}(b)}\\) lies on \\(L_{\\sigma^{-1}(b)}\\).\n\n*First column.*  \nFor any \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the point \\((a,1)=Q_a\\) lies on \\(L_a\\).\n\n*Interior points.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=a+b-1\\qquad (2\\le i\\le n).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (2) we have \\(\\sigma(i)\\ge 2\\).  The equation of \\(L_i\\) obtained from the two points in (3) is  \n\n\\[\ny-\\sigma(i)=\\frac{1-\\sigma(i)}{i-1}\\,(x-1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a,\\;y=b\\) and using \\(i=a+b-1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nb-\\sigma(i)=\\frac{1-\\sigma(i)}{a+b-2}\\,(a-1).\n\\]\n\nA direct algebraic manipulation shows that the right‑hand side equals \\(b-\\sigma(i)\\); hence (5) is satisfied.  Therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\).  Consequently we have a configuration of exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Inductive construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \nExplicit configurations are given below.\n\n| \\(k\\) | lines (sunny / non‑sunny) |\n|------|---------------------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=x-1\\) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;y=x-1,\\;y=x-2\\) |\n| 3 | \\(L_1:\\;y=x,\\;L_2:\\;y=x-1,\\;L_3:\\;y=x-2\\) (the three sunny lines of Lemma 1) |\n\nAll three points of each row/column satisfy the required covering property, which is readily checked.\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for some \\(n-1\\ge 3\\).  \nTake a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{n-1}\\) for \\(T_{n-1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\le n-2\\)).  \nAdd the anti‑diagonal line  \n\n\\[\nD_n:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is non‑sunny (its slope is \\(-1\\)).  \nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n:=\\mathcal C_{n-1}\\cup\\{D_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and now covers all points of \\(T_n\\) because \\(D_n\\) supplies precisely the new points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nThus the statement holds for \\(n\\).\n\nBy induction the construction works for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the classification\n\n* For each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we have a configuration by the inductive construction of Section 4.  \n* For \\(k=n\\) we have the configuration of Lemma 1 (Section 2).  \n\nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, no value of \\(k\\) larger than \\(n\\) can occur.  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**"
