\\).  To obtain a covering for *all* interior points we modify the permutation as follows.\n\n   Define a new permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma(i)=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   a & \\text{if }i=a+b-1\\text{ for some }a,b\\ge 2,\\\\[2pt]\n   \\text{the unique remaining element} & \\text{otherwise}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Because each integer \\(i\\in\\{3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can be written uniquely as \\(i=a+b-1\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) (take any \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le i-1\\) and set \\(b=i+1-a\\)), the rule (4) indeed defines a bijection; moreover \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq i\\) for every \\(i\\ge 2\\) (the equality would require \\(a=i\\) and \\(b=1\\), contradicting \\(b\\ge 2\\)).  \n\n   Replace the lines \\(L_i\\) of Lemma 1 by the lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde L_i:\\;\\text{the line through }(i,1)\\text{ and }(1,\\sigma(i))\\qquad (i=2,\\dots ,n),\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   keeping \\(L_0\\) unchanged.  The same argument as in Lemma 1 shows that each \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) is sunny and distinct.\n\n   For an interior point \\((a,b)\\) let \\(i=a+b-1\\).  By construction \\(\\sigma(i)=a\\).  The line \\(\\widetilde L_i\\) passes through \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,a)\\); its equation is  \n\n   \\[\n   (y-1)(1-i)=(a-1)(x-i).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(x=a,\\;y=b\\) and using \\(i=a+b-1\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   (b-1)(1-(a+b-1))=(a-1)(a-(a+b-1))\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Longleftrightarrow\\;(b-1)(2-a-b)=(a-1)(1-b),\n   \\]\n\n   which is an identity.  Hence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies (6) and lies on \\(\\widetilde L_i\\).\n\n   Consequently every interior point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\widetilde L_i\\).\n\nCombining the three cases, every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{L_0,\\widetilde L_2,\\dots ,\\widetilde L_n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Inductive construction for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \nExplicit configurations are listed below; each satisfies the covering condition and contains the required number of sunny lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | lines (sunny / non‑sunny) |\n|------|---------------------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=x-1\\) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;y=x-1,\\;y=x-2\\) |\n| 3 | the three sunny lines of Lemma 2 for \\(n=3\\) (e.g. \\(y=x,\\;y=x-1,\\;y=x-2\\)) |\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement holds for some \\(n-1\\ge 3\\).  Let \\(\\mathcal C_{n-1}\\) be a configuration for \\(T_{n-1}\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\le n-2\\)).  Add the line  \n\n\\[\nD_n:\\;x+y=n+1,\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is **non‑sunny**.  The new set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_n:=\\mathcal C_{n-1}\\cup\\{D_n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n\\) distinct lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and now covers all points of \\(T_n\\): the points already covered by \\(\\mathcal C_{n-1}\\) are those with \\(a+b\\le n\\); the newly added line \\(D_n\\) covers precisely the points with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  Hence the inductive claim holds for \\(n\\).\n\nBy induction, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the classification\n\n* For each \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) we obtain a configuration by the inductive procedure of Section 3.  \n* For \\(k=n\\) we use the sunny configuration supplied by Lemma 2.  \n\nSince we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, no value of \\(k\\) larger than \\(n\\) can occur.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the problem is completely solved.**"
