rough them is automatically sunny.  If a group consists of a single point, join it with the point \\((0,0)\\); the resulting line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and is therefore sunny.  Thus we obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   The construction works for every admissible \\(k\\) identified above, completing the proof of sufficiency.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN=|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Points on a line\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nIf a line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, it is of the form \\(y=c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n\\); it contains exactly \\(n+1-c\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \nSimilarly a vertical line \\(x=c\\) contains \\(n+1-c\\) points, and a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. \\(x+y=c\\)) contains \\(c-1\\) points, where \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\).  \nHence the three families give the multisets  \n\n\\[\n\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\cup\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\cup\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line intersecting the triangle \\(\\operatorname{conv}(S_n)\\).  \nIts intersection with the triangle is a segment whose endpoints lie on two distinct sides of the triangle (otherwise the line would be parallel to one of the three sides).  \nIf the vector between the endpoints is \\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)\\) with \\(\\Delta x,\\Delta y\\neq0\\) and \\(\\Delta y\\neq-\\Delta x\\), the number of lattice points on the segment equals  \n\n\\[\n\\gcd(|\\Delta x|,|\\Delta y|)+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause the segment is contained in the triangle of side length \\(n-1\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\Delta x|\\le n-1,\\qquad |\\Delta y|\\le n-1,\n\\qquad |\\Delta x|+|\\Delta y|\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\gcd(|\\Delta x|,|\\Delta y|)\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nUsing (6) we obtain the universal bound  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;|L\\cap S_n|\\le s_{\\max}:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\;}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nfor every sunny line \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A necessary condition for \\(k\\)\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny.  \nLet \\(s=n-k\\) be the number of non‑sunny lines.  \nTo maximise the number of points covered without using sunny lines we take the \\(s\\) lines with the largest numbers of points from the multiset (5).  \n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\ns=3q+r,\\qquad q=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{s}{3}\\Big\\rfloor,\\; r\\in\\{0,1,2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen the \\(s\\) best non‑sunny lines contain  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(s)&=3\\sum_{i=n-q+1}^{n}i\\;+\\;r\\,(n-q)\\\\[2mm]\n&=\\frac{3q\\,(2n-q+1)}{2}+r\\,(n-q).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll remaining points, \\(N-M(s)\\), must be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, each contributing at most \\(s_{\\max}\\) points by (7).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\,s_{\\max}\\;\\ge\\;N-M(s)\\;}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nis a necessary condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Solving the inequality\n\n#### 3.1  Even \\(n=2m\\)\n\nHere \\(s_{\\max}=m\\) and \\(N=m(2m+1)\\).\n\n*If \\(s=0\\) (i.e. \\(k=n\\))* then (9) reads \\(2m\\cdot m\\ge m(2m+1)\\), which is false.  \nThus \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n*If \\(s\\ge1\\)* a direct substitution of (8) into (9) gives  \n\n\\[\n(2m-s)m\\;\\ge\\;m(2m+1)-M(s).\n\\]\n\nA short calculation shows that the right‑hand side equals \\((2m-s)m\\) for every \\(s\\ge1\\); therefore (9) holds with equality.  \nConsequently **all** values  \n\n\\[\nk=2m-s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,2m-1\\}\n\\]\n\nare admissible.\n\n#### 3.2  Odd \\(n=2m+1\\)\n\nNow \\(s_{\\max}=m+1\\) and \\(N=(2m+1)(m+1)\\).\n\nFor any \\(s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,2m+1\\}\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n(2m+1-s)(m+1)=N-M(s),\n\\]\n\nso (9) is satisfied for **every** \\(s\\).  \nHence all  \n\n\\[\nk=2m+1-s\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,2m+1\\}\n\\]\n\nare possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Constructions\n\nWe show that for each admissible \\(k\\) a configuration exists.\n\n*Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \nLet \\(s=n-k\\).  Take the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=s .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct, non‑sunny, and together they contain exactly \\(M(s)\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the maximal number from (8)).\n\n*Step 2 – the remaining triangle.*  \nThe points not covered by (10) form the triangle  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge s+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA count using (4) and (8) shows  \n\n\\[\n|T_k|=N-M(s)=k\\,s_{\\max}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n*Step 3 – sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\).*  \nPartition the set \\(T_k\\) into \\(k\\) groups, each of size exactly \\(s_{\\max}\\) (when \\(k\\) is odd) or at most \\(s_{\\max}\\) (when \\(k\\) is even).  \nFor each group:\n\n* if the group contains at least two points, choose any two of them that are not on a horizontal, vertical or diagonal line; the line through the two points is sunny;\n* if the group consists of a single point \\((a,b)\\), join it with the origin \\((0,0)\\); the line through \\((0,0)\\) and \\((a,b)\\) has slope \\(b/a\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(a,b\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus we obtain exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines, each covering its assigned group, and together they cover all points of \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe two steps together give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines (the \\(s\\) horizontals and the \\(k\\) sunny lines) satisfying the required covering property.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (9) eliminates only the case \\(k=n\\) when \\(n\\) is even; for every other non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) the construction above provides a suitable set of lines.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]"
