eratorname{conv}(S_n)\\).  \nIts intersection with the triangle is a segment whose endpoints lie on two distinct sides of the triangle; otherwise \\(L\\) would be parallel to a side and thus non‑sunny.\n\nWrite the vector between the two endpoints as \\((\\Delta x,\\Delta y)\\) with \\(\\Delta x,\\Delta y\\neq0\\) and \\(\\Delta y\\neq-\\Delta x\\).  \nThe number of lattice points on the segment equals \\(\\gcd(|\\Delta x|,|\\Delta y|)+1\\) (standard lattice‑segment theorem).  \nBecause the segment is contained in a right‑isosceles triangle of leg length \\(n\\), we have  \n\n\\[\n|\\Delta x|\\le n-1,\\qquad |\\Delta y|\\le n-1,\\qquad |\\Delta x|+|\\Delta y|\\le n-1 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\gcd(|\\Delta x|,|\\Delta y|)\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nTherefore every sunny line satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|L\\cap S_n|\\le s_{\\max}:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor } .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nRemove the \\(k\\) sunny lines; the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.  \n\nChoose the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny and they cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  \nThe uncovered points are precisely\n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a,\\;b-(n-k))\\) sends \\(T_k\\) bijectively onto  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nthe right‑isosceles lattice triangle of size \\(k\\).  \nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines must cover all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nInside the triangle \\(S_k\\) the bound (4) becomes  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S_k|\\le \\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|S_k|=k(k+1)/2\\), the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover \\(S_k\\) only if  \n\n\\[\nk\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k\\) is odd, \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) and (7) holds with equality.  \nIf \\(k\\) is even, \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=\\frac{k}{2}\\) and the left‑hand side of (7) equals \\(\\frac{k^{2}}2<\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\), a contradiction.  \n\nHence a **necessary** condition is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd}} .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Sufficiency\n\nWe prove that every \\(k\\) satisfying (8) can indeed be realised.\n\n*Case \\(k=0\\).*  \nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\).  \nAll are non‑sunny and together they contain every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Case \\(k\\) odd, \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).*  \nPut \\(s=n-k\\).  \n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Use the \\(s\\) horizontal lines (5).  \n   They cover all points of \\(S_n\\) with second coordinate \\(\\le s\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   The uncovered points form the translate of \\(S_k\\).  \n   Because \\(k\\) is odd, equality holds in (7); consequently each sunny line may contain exactly \\(\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) points of \\(S_k\\).\n\n   For each \\(r\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   P_r=(r,\\;k+1-r),\\qquad Q_r=(r+1,\\;k-r).\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(L_r\\) through \\(P_r\\) and \\(Q_r\\) has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   m_r=\\frac{(k-r)-(k+1-r)}{(r+1)-r}=-1+\\frac{1}{r+1},\n   \\]\n\n   which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(L_r\\) is sunny.  \n   The \\(k\\) lines \\(\\{L_r\\}_{r=1}^{k}\\) are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n   A direct verification shows that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(L_r\\):\n   * if \\(a+b=k+1\\) then \\((a,b)=P_a\\) and belongs to \\(L_a\\);\n   * if \\(a+b\\le k\\) then \\((a,b)=Q_{a-1}\\) (with the convention \\(Q_0=(1,k)\\)) and belongs to \\(L_{a-1}\\).\n\n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_k\\).\n\nCombining the \\(s\\) horizontal (non‑sunny) lines with the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) yields \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the whole set \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus every \\(k\\) satisfying (8) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary condition (8) is also sufficient.  \nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd},\\ 1\\le k\\le n\\; } .\n\\]"
