on.\n\n2. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Diagonal (slope\u2011\\(1\\)) lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(r\\) with \\(-\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\le r\\le\n   \\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\rfloor\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_r:\\;y=x+r .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_r\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(L_r\\) is sunny.\n   Its intersection with \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   L_r\\cap S_n=\\{(i,i+r)\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\},\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(m\\) is given by (1).  Consequently each \\(L_r\\) contains exactly\n   \\(m\\) points of \\(S_n\\), and the families \\(\\{L_r\\cap S_n\\}\\) are pairwise\n   disjoint.\n\n   *Horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines.*  \n   The \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   H_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   are all non\u2011sunny and together cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Replacing horizontals by diagonals.*  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the bound (4).  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct values \\(r_1,\\dots ,r_k\\) from the range of (5) and\n   replace the horizontal lines \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_k\\) by the sunny lines\n   \\(L_{r_1},\\dots ,L_{r_k}\\).  \n   Because of (6), each point that belonged to a deleted horizontal line\n   still lies on the newly inserted diagonal line with the same abscissa,\n   so every point of \\(S_n\\) remains covered.  \n   The total number of lines stays equal to \\(n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are\n   sunny.  Hence a configuration exists for every \\(k\\) that satisfies (4).\n\n   *Extremal cases.*  \n\n   - **\\(n\\) odd.**  Write \\(n=2t+1\\); then \\(m=t+1\\) and \\(|S_n|=n\\,m\\).\n     The \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{\\,L_i:\\;y=x+(i-t-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,}\n     \\tag{8}\n     \\]\n\n     are pairwise disjoint, each contains exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(S_n\\), and\n     their union is \\(S_n\\).  Thus a covering with **all** \\(n\\) lines sunny\n     exists.\n\n   - **\\(n\\) even.**  Write \\(n=2t\\); then \\(m=t\\) and \\(|S_n|=n\\,t\\).\n     The \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines \\(L_i\\) given by (8) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)\n     cover all points of \\(S_n\\) except those on the horizontal line\n     \\(y=t\\).  Adding this horizontal line (non\u2011sunny) yields a covering with\n     \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Consequently, for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) we have exhibited a\n   concrete configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny\n   ones that covers all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nWrite a non\u2011vertical line in the integer form  \n\n\\[\nax+by=c,\\qquad a,b,c\\in\\mathbb Z,\\; \\gcd(a,b)=1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe line is sunny exactly when  \n\n\\[\nab\\neq0,\\qquad a\\neq -b .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nAll lattice points on the line are obtained from one particular solution\n\\((x_0,y_0)\\) by adding integer multiples of the vector \\((b,-a)\\):\n\\[\n(x,y)=(x_0,y_0)+t\\,(b,-a),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nHence, moving from one lattice point to the next changes  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta x = b,\\qquad \\Delta y = -a,\\qquad\n\\Delta (x+y)=b-a .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (11) we have \\(|b|\\ge 1\\) and \\(|b-a|\\ge 1\\).\n\nLet \\((x_0,y_0)\\) be the lattice point of the line with the smallest\nabscissa that belongs to \\(S_n\\) (if none exists the line contributes no\npoint).  Starting from \\((x_0,y_0)\\) and applying (12) repeatedly we obtain\nall lattice points of the line that lie in \\(S_n\\).  After \\(t\\) steps we have  \n\n\\[\nx = x_0 + t b,\\qquad\nx+y = (x_0+y_0) + t(b-a).\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nBoth coordinates must satisfy the restrictions defining \\(S_n\\):\n\\(1\\le x\\le n\\) and \\(2\\le x+y\\le n+1\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nt\\le \\frac{n-x_0}{|b|},\\qquad\nt\\le \\frac{n+1-(x_0+y_0)}{|b-a|}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|b|\\ge 1\\) and \\(|b-a|\\ge 1\\), each of the two right\u2013hand sides is at\nmost \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Therefore the number of\ninteger points of a sunny line that belong to \\(S_n\\) is bounded by  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nA non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal (\\(y=c\\)), vertical (\\(x=c\\)) or of\nslope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=c\\)).  In each of these three families the inequality\n\\(x+y\\le n+1\\) together with \\(1\\le x,y\\le n\\) shows that at most \\(n\\) points\nof \\(S_n\\) can lie on such a line.\n\nAssume a configuration consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\nlines.  Using the above bounds, the total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that can\nbe covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\,m+(n-k)\\,n .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nSince every point of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, we need  \n\n\\[\nk\\,m+(n-k)\\,n\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nInsert the value of \\(m\\) from (16).\n\n*If \\(n=2t\\) (even), then \\(m=t\\).  Inequality (18) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk\\,t+(2t-k)\\,2t\\ge t(2t+1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow t(2t-k-1)\\ge0,\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2t-1=n-1 .\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(n=2t+1\\) (odd), then \\(m=t+1\\).  Inequality (18) becomes  \n\n\\[\nk(t+1)+(2t+1-k)(2t+1)\\ge (2t+1)(t+1)\n\\Longleftrightarrow t(2t+1-k)\\ge0,\n\\]\n\nso  \n\n\\[\nk\\le 2t+1=n .\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nThus a **necessary** condition for the existence of a required family of\n\\(n\\) lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\nk\\le n-1 &\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\\nk\\le n   &\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions that meet the bound  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Diagonal (slope\u2011\\(1\\)) lines  \n\nFor each integer  \n\n\\[\nr\\in\\Bigl\\{-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\\;\n-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1,\\dots ,\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\n\nlet  \n\n\\[\nL_r:\\;y=x+r .\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_r\\) is \\(1\\); therefore \\(L_r\\) is sunny.\nA direct verification shows that  \n\n\\[\nL_r\\cap S_n=\\{(i,i+r)\\mid 1\\le i\\le m\\},\n\\tag{25}\n\\]\n\nso each \\(L_r\\) contains exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(S_n\\), and the sets\n\\(L_r\\cap S_n\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Horizontal (non\u2011sunny) lines  \n\nThe \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_b:\\;y=b\\qquad(b=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{26}\n\\]\n\nare all non\u2011sunny and together cover the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Obtaining any admissible number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy the bound (21).\n\n*Choose \\(k\\) distinct values \\(r_1,\\dots ,r_k\\) from the range (23) and\nreplace the horizontal lines \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_k\\) by the sunny lines\n\\(L_{r_1},\\dots ,L_{r_k}\\).*  \n\nBecause of (25), each point that lay on a deleted horizontal line \\(H_j\\)\nhas abscissa \\(j\\) and therefore belongs to the unique diagonal line\n\\(L_{r_i}\\) with \\(r_i=j-i\\); consequently it remains covered.  The\nremaining \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines still cover all points that are not on any\nof the inserted diagonals.  Hence we obtain a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct\nlines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus a configuration exists for **every** integer \\(k\\) that satisfies (21).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  Extremal values  \n\n*Odd \\(n\\).*  Write \\(n=2t+1\\).  Then \\(m=t+1\\) and \\(|S_n|=n\\,m\\).\nThe \\(n\\) diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,L_i:\\;y=x+(i-t-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\,}\n\\tag{27}\n\\]\n\nare pairwise disjoint, each contains exactly \\(m\\) points of \\(S_n\\), and\ntheir union is \\(S_n\\).  Hence a covering with **all** \\(n\\) lines sunny\nexists, showing that the bound \\(k\\le n\\) is sharp for odd \\(n\\).\n\n*Even \\(n\\).*  Write \\(n=2t\\).  Then \\(m=t\\) and \\(|S_n|=n\\,t\\).\nThe \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines \\(L_i\\) given by (27) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\)\ncover all points of \\(S_n\\) except those on the horizontal line \\(y=t\\).\nAdding this horizontal line (non\u2011sunny) yields a covering with exactly\n\\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines, proving that the bound \\(k\\le n-1\\) is sharp for even\n\\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nThe inequality (21) is necessary, and the constructions of Sections\u202f2.3\u20132.4\nshow that it is also sufficient.  Therefore the set of all possible numbers\nof sunny lines is exactly the one stated in the answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
