-t+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }1),\\qquad \n> V_t:\\;x=t\\qquad(\\text{vertical}).\n> \\]\n\n> Then the set \\(\\{S_t,V_t\\}\\) covers exactly the same points of \\(T_n\\) as\n> the single line \\(H_t\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) lies on \\(H_t\\) iff \\(b=t\\).  \nIf \\(b=t\\) then either \\(a\\le t\\) or \\(a>t\\).\n\n* If \\(a\\le t\\) we have \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t\\}\\); consequently the vertical line\n  \\(V_t:x=t\\) contains the point \\((a,b)\\) because \\(a=t\\) is false but the\n  point \\((t,b)\\) belongs to \\(V_t\\) and coincides with \\((a,b)\\) only when\n  \\(a=t\\).  In the case \\(a<t\\) the point \\((a,b)\\) is covered by the sunny\n  line \\(S_t\\): indeed \\(b=t = a-t+1\\) is equivalent to \\(a-b=t-1\\), i.e.\n  \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,t-1\\}\\), which holds because \\(a\\le t\\).\n\n* If \\(a>t\\) then \\(a-b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a-b\\le n+1-2t\\le t-1\\) (the last\n  inequality follows from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  Hence \\(a-b\\in\\{0,\\dots ,t-1\\}\\)\n  and the point lies on the sunny line \\(S_t\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) that was on \\(H_t\\) is still covered by\n\\(\\{S_t,V_t\\}\\).  Conversely, any point on \\(S_t\\) or on \\(V_t\\) satisfies\n\\(b=t\\) or \\(a=t\\); in both cases the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces\n\\(b=t\\).  Hence the point also belongs to \\(H_t\\).  \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nApply Lemma\u202f2.1 to the horizontal lines \\(H_1,\\dots ,H_k\\) and keep the\nremaining horizontal lines \\(H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\) unchanged.\nAfter the replacements we have the following family of \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n  \\[\n  H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\qquad (y=t,\\;t=k+1,\\dots ,n);\n  \\]\n\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  S_t:\\;y=x-t+1\\qquad (t=1,\\dots ,k);\n  \\]\n\n* the \\(k\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  V_t:\\;x=t\\qquad (t=1,\\dots ,k).\n  \\]\n\nAll three groups are pairwise disjoint, so the total number of lines is\n\\((n-k)+k+k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Verification that the family covers \\(T_n\\)\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\ge k+1\\)*, the horizontal line \\(H_b\\) (which is present) contains\n\\((a,b)\\).\n\n*If \\(1\\le b\\le k\\)*, two sub\u2011cases occur.\n\n1. **\\(a\\le b\\).**  \n   Then \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and the vertical line \\(V_a:x=a\\) contains the\n   point.\n\n2. **\\(a>b\\).**  \n   Then \\(a-b\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\); consequently the sunny line\n   \\(S_b:\\;y=x-b+1\\) passes through \\((a,b)\\).\n\nIn every possible situation the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on at least one of the\n\\(n\\) constructed lines.  Hence the covering condition of the problem is\nsatisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  Conversely, the construction for \\(k=0\\) shows that no\nadditional restriction on \\(k\\) is necessary.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]"
