ertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); none of these slopes is allowed for a sunny line.  Hence each of the three points would require a distinct sunny line, contradicting the hypothesis that only two sunny lines are present.\n\n3. **Why the previous \u201creplacement lemma\u2019\u2019 fails.**  \n   The lemma claimed that the horizontal line \\(y=t\\) could be replaced by the two lines \\(y=x-t+1\\) (slope\u202f1) and \\(x=t\\) (vertical) without losing any covered points of \\(T_n\\).  In fact the point \\((t+1,t)\\in T_n\\) lies on the original horizontal line but lies on neither of the two replacement lines, showing the claim to be false.\n\nThese observations settle the two extremal cases and rule out the value \\(k=2\\) for all sufficiently large \\(n\\).  Determining the exact set of admissible values of \\(k\\) for a general \\(n\\) remains an open problem."

