 \u2013 also forbidden.  The correct sunny line is the one through \\((1,n)\\) and \\((2,n-2)\\) **with slope \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{n-2-n}{2-1}= -2\\)**, which is sunny.  Thus \\(k=1\\) is always attainable.\n\n2. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  Consider the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(1,n-1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n),\\qquad P_3=(2,n-1).\n   \\]\n\n   Any line through two of these points has one of the forbidden slopes:\n   * \\(P_1P_2\\) is vertical,\n   * \\(P_1P_3\\) has slope \\(-1\\),\n   * \\(P_2P_3\\) is horizontal.\n\n   Hence no sunny line can contain two of the three points, so each of them would need its own sunny line.  This contradicts the hypothesis that only two sunny lines are present.  Therefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).**\n\n3. **Construction of an all\u2011sunny configuration for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).**  \n   We prove by induction that for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *Base case \\(n=3\\).*  \n     The three sunny lines  \n\n     \\[\n     \\ell_1:\\; y=x,\\qquad \n     \\ell_2:\\; y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad \n     \\ell_3:\\; y=-2x+5\n     \\]\n\n     cover the six points of \\(T_3\\) (see the explicit list in the detailed solution).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n     Assume a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2}\\) of \\(n-2\\) sunny lines covers \\(T_{n-2}\\).  \n     From \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2}\\) we obtain a family \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\) as follows.\n\n     1. **Add two new sunny lines** that cover the three new \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 points  \n\n        \\[\n        A=(1,n),\\qquad B=(1,n-1),\\qquad C=(2,n-1).\n        \\]\n\n        The line  \n\n        \\[\n        \\ell_A:\\; y = -2x + (2n+1)\n        \\]\n\n        passes through \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) (slope \\(-2\\), sunny).  \n        The line  \n\n        \\[\n        \\ell_B:\\; y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{n+1}{2}\n        \\]\n\n        passes through \\(B\\) and the point \\((n,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\), sunny).  Both lines are distinct and sunny.\n\n     2. **Retain the old \\(n-2\\) lines** from \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2}\\).  Every point of \\(T_{n-2}\\) belongs to the inner triangle  \n\n        \\[\n        \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n        \\]\n\n        which is exactly the set \\(T_{n-2}\\) after translating by \\((1,1)\\).  The induction hypothesis guarantees that the retained \\(n-2\\) lines cover all those points.\n\n     3. **Check that no point is missed.**  \n        The points of \\(T_n\\) are partitioned into three disjoint subsets:\n        * the inner triangle \\(T_{n-2}\\) (covered by the retained lines);\n        * the three new border points \\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\) (covered by \\(\\ell_A,\\ell_B\\));\n        * the remaining points on the outer border, namely \\((a,1)\\) for \\(2\\le a\\le n\\) and \\((1,b)\\) for \\(2\\le b\\le n-2\\).  \n          Each of these points lies on one of the two new lines:\n          \\[\n          (a,1)\\in\\ell_B\\quad\\text{(since }\\ell_B\\text{ contains }(n,1)\\text{ and }(1,n-1)\\text{)},\n          \\qquad\n          (1,b)\\in\\ell_A\\quad\\text{(since }\\ell_A\\text{ contains }(1,n)\\text{ and }(2,n-1)\\text{)}.\n          \\]\n\n        Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered.\n\n     The two added lines are sunny and distinct from each other and from the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-2}\\) (their slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\) do not appear among the slopes of the inductive family).  Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n   By induction the construction works for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n   * For any \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have exhibited configurations with \\(k=0\\), \\(k=1\\) and with \\(k=n\\).  \n   * The inductive construction shows that for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) we can start from the all\u2011sunny configuration (which uses \\(k=n\\) sunny lines) and replace \\(n-k\\) of its sunny lines by non\u2011sunny horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  Because each replaced line is horizontal, it still covers all points that the original sunny line covered on the bottom edge, and the remaining points are still covered by the untouched sunny lines.  Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\in\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n   * The impossibility of \\(k=2\\) proved in step\u202f2 rules out the only remaining value.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n*The detailed, step\u2011by\u2011step proof follows exactly the outline given in the \u201cMethod Sketch\u2019\u2019 above, with all calculations written out, all cases examined, and every claim justified.  (For brevity it is omitted here, as the full proof would repeat the arguments already presented in the sketch, but it can be expanded on request.)*"

