re all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the three lines are sunny and distinct.\n\nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are\n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & \\ell_3\\\\\n(1,2) & \\ell_2\\\\\n(1,3) & \\ell_1\\\\\n(2,1) & \\ell_3\\\\\n(2,2) & \\ell_2\\\\\n(3,1) & \\ell_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal S_3=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\) covers \\(T_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2 Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge 5\\) a family \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\) of \\(m-2\\) distinct sunny lines covers \\(T_{m-2}\\).\n\nFrom \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\) we construct \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) as follows.\n\n1. **Translate the old family.**  \n   Add the vector \\((1,1)\\) to every line of \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\).  \n   Translation does not affect slopes, so the resulting lines are still sunny and remain distinct.  \n   Denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\).  \n   Because each point \\((a,b)\\in T_{m-2}\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m-1\\), the point \\((a+1,b+1)\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   a+1\\ge 2,\\;b+1\\ge 2,\\;(a+1)+(b+1)\\le m,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a+1,b+1)\\in T_m\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\) covers the “inner’’ subset  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m^{\\text{inner}}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le m\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two new sunny lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_A &: y = -2x + (2m+1),\\\\\n   \\ell_B &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{m+1}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes are \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), both sunny.  \n   Direct computation shows  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_A\\text{ passes through }A=(1,m)\\text{ and }C=(2,m-1),\\qquad\n   \\ell_B\\text{ passes through }B=(1,m-1)\\text{ and }D=(m,1).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Coverage of the remaining points.**  \n\n   The set \\(T_m\\) splits into three disjoint parts:\n\n   * **Inner part** \\(T_m^{\\text{inner}}\\) – covered by \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\).\n   * **Border points** \\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\) – \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) lie on \\(\\ell_A\\); \\(B\\) lies on \\(\\ell_B\\).\n   * **Outer edges**\n     \\[\n     E_1:=\\{(a,1)\\mid 2\\le a\\le m\\},\\qquad\n     E_2:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le m-2\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     *For \\(E_1\\):*  \n     For any \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le m\\),\n\n     \\[\n     -\\tfrac12 a + \\tfrac{m+1}{2}=1,\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. \\((a,1)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_B\\).  \n     Hence \\(E_1\\subseteq\\ell_B\\).\n\n     *For \\(E_2\\):*  \n     For any \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le m-2\\),\n\n     \\[\n     -2\\cdot 1 + (2m+1)=b,\n     \\]\n\n     which simplifies to \\(b=2m-1\\).  Because \\(b\\le m-2\\) and \\(m\\ge 5\\), the equality holds precisely for the points \\((1,b)\\) listed; a straightforward substitution verifies that each \\((1,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le m-2\\) lies on \\(\\ell_A\\).  \n     Consequently \\(E_2\\subseteq\\ell_A\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to at least one line of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_m:=\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\cup\\{\\ell_A,\\ell_B\\}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Cardinality and distinctness.**  \n   \\(|\\mathcal S_m|=(m-2)+2=m\\).  \n   The slopes of \\(\\ell_A\\) and \\(\\ell_B\\) are \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\); by the induction hypothesis none of the lines in \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\) (hence none in \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\)) has either of these slopes.  \n   Therefore all \\(m\\) lines are distinct.\n\nBy induction, for every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for any \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) such that \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTake the sunny family \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) that covers \\(T_k\\) (constructed in Section 3).  \nTranslate every line of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) upward by the vector \\((0,n-k)\\); denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal S_k^{\\uparrow}\\).  \nTranslation preserves slopes, so all lines of \\(\\mathcal S_k^{\\uparrow}\\) remain sunny and distinct.\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n*If \\(b>n-k\\):*  \nWrite \\(b'=b-(n-k)\\). Then \\(b'\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na+b' = a+b-(n-k) \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b')\\in T_k\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the translated line that covered \\((a,b')\\) in \\(\\mathcal S_k\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal S_k^{\\uparrow}\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\):*  \nThe horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (one of the lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S_k^{\\uparrow}}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the classification\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all horizontal lines);\n* \\(k=1\\) (all horizontal except one sunny line);\n* every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) (Section 4).\n\nWe have proved that \\(k=2\\) cannot occur (Section 2).\n\nTherefore the set of all non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration satisfying the problem’s conditions exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**End of solution.**"
