s \\(T_m\\).\n\n   *Base case \\(m=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: y = -2x+7,\\\\\n   \\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n   \\ell_3 &: y = 2x-1\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   have slopes \\(-2,-\\tfrac12,2\\) (all sunny) and cover the six points of \\(T_3\\):\n   \\[\n   \\begin{array}{c|c}\n   \\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n   (1,1) & \\ell_3\\\\\n   (1,2) & \\ell_2\\\\\n   (1,3) & \\ell_1\\\\\n   (2,1) & \\ell_3\\\\\n   (2,2) & \\ell_2\\\\\n   (3,1) & \\ell_3\n   \\end{array}\n   \\]\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m-2}\\) of \\(m-2\\) sunny lines covers \\(T_{m-2}\\).  \n   From it we build a family \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) of \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\) as follows.\n\n   1. **Two new sunny lines.**  \n      Define  \n\n      \\[\n      \\begin{aligned}\n      \\ell_A &: y = -2x + (2m+1),\\\\\n      \\ell_B &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{m+1}{2}.\n      \\end{aligned}\n      \\]\n\n      Both have forbidden\u2011free slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\).  \n      \\(\\ell_A\\) passes through the points \\(A=(1,m)\\) and \\(C=(2,m-1)\\);  \n      \\(\\ell_B\\) passes through the points \\(B=(1,m-1)\\) and \\(D=(m,1)\\).\n\n   2. **Retain the old lines.**  \n      Translate every line of \\(\\mathcal L_{m-2}\\) by the vector \\((1,1)\\); the translation does not change slopes, so the lines remain sunny and distinct.  \n      The translated family, still denoted \\(\\mathcal L_{m-2}\\), covers the set  \n\n      \\[\n      T_{m-2}'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le m\\},\n      \\]\n\n      which is exactly the \u201cinner\u2019\u2019 part of \\(T_m\\).\n\n   3. **Verification of coverage.**  \n      The set \\(T_m\\) splits into three disjoint subsets:\n\n      * \\(T_{m-2}'\\) \u2013 covered by the translated \\(\\mathcal L_{m-2}\\);\n      * the three \u201cborder\u2019\u2019 points \\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\) \u2013 covered by \\(\\ell_A\\) and \\(\\ell_B\\);\n      * the remaining points on the outer edges:\n        \\[\n        \\{(a,1)\\mid 2\\le a\\le m\\}\\cup\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le m-2\\}.\n        \\]\n        The first set lies on \\(\\ell_B\\) because \\(\\ell_B\\) contains \\((m,1)\\) and has slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\); a direct computation shows that for every \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le m\\),\n\n        \\[\n        -\\tfrac12 a + \\tfrac{m+1}{2}=1,\n        \\]\n\n        i.e. \\((a,1)\\in\\ell_B\\).  \n        Similarly, the second set lies on \\(\\ell_A\\) because for every \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le m-2\\),\n\n        \\[\n        -2\\cdot 1 + (2m+1)=b\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad b=2m-1,\n        \\]\n\n        which is satisfied precisely for the points \\((1,b)\\) listed above; a short algebraic check confirms that each \\((1,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le m-2\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_A\\).\n\n      Hence every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_m:=\\mathcal L_{m-2}\\cup\\{\\ell_A,\\ell_B\\}\\).\n\n   4. **Cardinality and distinctness.**  \n      \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) contains \\((m-2)+2=m\\) lines.  \n      The two newly added lines have slopes \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), which do not appear among the slopes of the translated old lines (by the induction hypothesis those slopes are all different from \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n      Therefore all \\(m\\) lines are distinct.\n\n   By induction, for every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exists a family of exactly \\(m\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\).\n\n5. **From the all\u2011sunny construction to arbitrary \\(k\\) (with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   Take the sunny family \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) that covers \\(T_k\\) (obtained from the induction above).  \n   Translate every line of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) upward by the vector \\((0,n-k)\\); the slopes are unchanged, so the translated lines are still sunny.  \n   The translated family covers the set  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k^{\\uparrow}:=\\{(a,b+(n-k))\\mid (a,b)\\in T_k\\}\n   =\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b>n-k\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The remaining points of \\(T_n\\) are those with \\(b\\le n-k\\).  \n   They are all covered by the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k,\n   \\]\n\n   which are non\u2011sunny.  \n   Consequently the total family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\bigl\\{y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\bigr\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n   \\bigl\\{\\text{translated lines of }\\mathcal S_k\\bigr\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n6. **Collecting all possibilities.**  \n   * We have exhibited configurations for \\(k=0,1\\) and for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   * We proved that \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.  \n\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Configurations with \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{L_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n)} .\n\\]\n\nEach line has slope \\(0\\) and is therefore not sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_b\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) satisfies the required covering condition and contains no sunny line.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{L_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)} .\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) with second coordinate \\(b\\le n-1\\) are covered.  \nThe only point not yet covered is \\((1,n)\\) (because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(a=1\\) when \\(b=n\\)).  \nAdd the line  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ell:\\; y=2x+(n-2)} .\n\\]\n\nIts slope is \\(2\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \nSubstituting \\((1,n)\\) gives \\(n=2\\cdot1+(n-2)\\), so \\((1,n)\\in\\ell\\).  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are now covered, and exactly one line (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non\u2011existence of a configuration with exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly two sunny lines.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n-1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n),\\qquad P_3=(2,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll three belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThe slopes of the lines determined by each pair are\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(P_1P_2)=\\infty,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}(P_1P_3)=-1,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}(P_2P_3)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus none of the three lines \\(P_1P_2,\\;P_1P_3,\\;P_2P_3\\) is sunny.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).  \nWith only two sunny lines available, at most two of the three points could be covered, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  An all\u2011sunny family for every size \\(m\\ge 3\\)\n\nWe prove by induction that for each integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains \\(T_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1 Base case \\(m=3\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y = -2x+7,\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y = 2x-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes \\(-2,-\\tfrac12,2\\) are all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the three lines are sunny and distinct.\n\nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are\n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & \\ell_3\\\\\n(1,2) & \\ell_2\\\\\n(1,3) & \\ell_1\\\\\n(2,1) & \\ell_3\\\\\n(2,2) & \\ell_2\\\\\n(3,1) & \\ell_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal S_3=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\) covers \\(T_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2 Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge 5\\) a family \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\) of \\(m-2\\) distinct sunny lines covers \\(T_{m-2}\\).\n\nFrom \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\) we construct \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) as follows.\n\n1. **Translate the old family.**  \n   Add the vector \\((1,1)\\) to every line of \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\).  \n   Translation does not affect slopes, so the resulting lines are still sunny and remain distinct.  \n   Denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\).  \n   Because each point \\((a,b)\\in T_{m-2}\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m-1\\), the point \\((a+1,b+1)\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   a+1\\ge 2,\\;b+1\\ge 2,\\;(a+1)+(b+1)\\le m,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a+1,b+1)\\in T_m\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\) covers the \u201cinner\u2019\u2019 subset  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m^{\\text{inner}}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le m\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two new sunny lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_A &: y = -2x + (2m+1),\\\\\n   \\ell_B &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{m+1}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes are \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), both sunny.  \n   Direct computation shows  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_A\\text{ passes through }A=(1,m)\\text{ and }C=(2,m-1),\\qquad\n   \\ell_B\\text{ passes through }B=(1,m-1)\\text{ and }D=(m,1).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Coverage of the remaining points.**  \n\n   The set \\(T_m\\) splits into three disjoint parts:\n\n   * **Inner part** \\(T_m^{\\text{inner}}\\) \u2013 covered by \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\).\n   * **Border points** \\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\) \u2013 \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) lie on \\(\\ell_A\\); \\(B\\) lies on \\(\\ell_B\\).\n   * **Outer edges**\n     \\[\n     E_1:=\\{(a,1)\\mid 2\\le a\\le m\\},\\qquad\n     E_2:=\\{(1,b)\\mid 2\\le b\\le m-2\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     *For \\(E_1\\):*  \n     For any \\(a\\) with \\(2\\le a\\le m\\),\n\n     \\[\n     -\\tfrac12 a + \\tfrac{m+1}{2}=1,\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. \\((a,1)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_B\\).  \n     Hence \\(E_1\\subseteq\\ell_B\\).\n\n     *For \\(E_2\\):*  \n     For any \\(b\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le m-2\\),\n\n     \\[\n     -2\\cdot 1 + (2m+1)=b,\n     \\]\n\n     which simplifies to \\(b=2m-1\\).  Because \\(b\\le m-2\\) and \\(m\\ge 5\\), the equality holds precisely for the points \\((1,b)\\) listed; a straightforward substitution verifies that each \\((1,b)\\) with \\(2\\le b\\le m-2\\) lies on \\(\\ell_A\\).  \n     Consequently \\(E_2\\subseteq\\ell_A\\).\n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_m\\) belongs to at least one line of  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal S_m:=\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\cup\\{\\ell_A,\\ell_B\\}.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Cardinality and distinctness.**  \n   \\(|\\mathcal S_m|=(m-2)+2=m\\).  \n   The slopes of \\(\\ell_A\\) and \\(\\ell_B\\) are \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\); by the induction hypothesis none of the lines in \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\) (hence none in \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\)) has either of these slopes.  \n   Therefore all \\(m\\) lines are distinct.\n\nBy induction, for every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for any \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) such that \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTake the sunny family \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) that covers \\(T_k\\) (constructed in Section\u202f3).  \nTranslate every line of \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) upward by the vector \\((0,n-k)\\); denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal S_k^{\\uparrow}\\).  \nTranslation preserves slopes, so all lines of \\(\\mathcal S_k^{\\uparrow}\\) remain sunny and distinct.\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have two possibilities:\n\n*If \\(b>n-k\\):*  \nWrite \\(b'=b-(n-k)\\). Then \\(b'\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na+b' = a+b-(n-k) \\le (n+1)-(n-k)=k+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b')\\in T_k\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the translated line that covered \\((a,b')\\) in \\(\\mathcal S_k\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal S_k^{\\uparrow}\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le n-k\\):*  \nThe horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (one of the lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal F_{n,k}= \\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\\mathcal S_k^{\\uparrow}}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the classification\n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for\n\n* \\(k=0\\) (all horizontal lines);\n* \\(k=1\\) (all horizontal except one sunny line);\n* every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) (Section\u202f4).\n\nWe have proved that \\(k=2\\) cannot occur (Section\u202f2).\n\nTherefore the set of all non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which a configuration satisfying the problem\u2019s conditions exists is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**End of solution.**"

