overed, and exactly one line (\\(\\ell\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non‑existence of a configuration with exactly two sunny lines\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) and containing exactly two sunny lines.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,n-1),\\qquad P_2=(1,n),\\qquad P_3=(2,n-1).\n\\]\n\nAll three belong to \\(T_n\\).  \nThe slopes of the lines determined by each pair are\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{slope}(P_1P_2)=\\infty,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}(P_1P_3)=-1,\\qquad\n\\operatorname{slope}(P_2P_3)=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus none of the three lines \\(P_1P_2,\\;P_1P_3,\\;P_2P_3\\) is sunny.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).  \nWith only two sunny lines available, at most two of the three points could be covered, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\).  \nHence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  An all‑sunny family for every size \\(m\\ge 3\\)\n\nWe prove by induction that for each integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) of exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains \\(T_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1 Base case \\(m=3\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y = -2x+7,\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y = 2x-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe slopes \\(-2,-\\tfrac12,2\\) are all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the three lines are sunny and distinct.\n\nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are\n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nA direct substitution shows\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{point} & \\text{line containing it}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & \\ell_3\\\\\n(1,2) & \\ell_2\\\\\n(1,3) & \\ell_1\\\\\n(2,1) & \\ell_3\\\\\n(2,2) & \\ell_2\\\\\n(3,1) & \\ell_3\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal S_3=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\) covers \\(T_3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2 Inductive step\n\nAssume that for some \\(m\\ge 5\\) a family \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\) of \\(m-2\\) distinct sunny lines covers \\(T_{m-2}\\).\n\nFrom \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\) we construct \\(\\mathcal S_m\\) as follows.\n\n1. **Translate the old family.**  \n   Add the vector \\((1,1)\\) to every line of \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}\\).  \n   Translation does not affect slopes, so the resulting lines are still sunny and remain distinct.  \n   Denote the translated family by \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\).  \n   Because each point \\((a,b)\\in T_{m-2}\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m-1\\), the point \\((a+1,b+1)\\) satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   a+1\\ge 2,\\;b+1\\ge 2,\\;(a+1)+(b+1)\\le m,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a+1,b+1)\\in T_m\\).  \n   Hence \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\) covers the “inner’’ subset  \n\n   \\[\n   T_m^{\\text{inner}}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le m\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Two new sunny lines.**  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_A &: y = -2x + (2m+1),\\\\\n   \\ell_B &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{m+1}{2}.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Their slopes are \\(-2\\) and \\(-\\tfrac12\\), both sunny.  \n   Direct computation shows  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_A\\text{ passes through }A=(1,m)\\text{ and }C=(2,m-1),\\qquad\n   \\ell_B\\text{ passes through }B=(1,m-1)\\text{ and }D=(m,1).\n   \\]\n\n3. **Coverage of the remaining points.**  \n\n   The set \\(T_m\\) splits into three disjoint parts:\n\n   * **Inner part** \\(T_m^{\\text{inner}}\\) – covered by \\(\\mathcal S_{m-2}'\\).\n   * **Border points** \\(\\{A,B,C\\}\\) – \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) lie on \\(\\ell_A\\); \\(B\\) lies on \\(\\ell_B\\).\n   * **Outer edges**\n