vers \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  General construction for arbitrary \\(k\\) (except the special case \\(n=3,k=2\\))\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(n\\ge4\\) (the case \\(n=3\\) will be treated separately).\n\n1. **Choice of rows.**  \n   Select \\(k\\) distinct integers  \n\n   \\[\n   b_1,b_2,\\dots ,b_k\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Choice of points.**  \n   For each \\(i\\) pick any integer \\(a_i\\) with \\(1\\le a_i\\le n+1-b_i\\); then  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(a_i,b_i)\\in T_n .\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny lines.**  \n   For each \\(i\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (b_i-i a_i).\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(i\\).  \n   Since \\(i\\ge 1\\) and \\(i\\neq 0,-1\\), each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The slopes are pairwise different, so the lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct.  \n   By construction each \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the chosen point \\(P_i\\).\n\n4. **Horizontal lines for the remaining rows.**  \n   For every row index \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_k\\}\\) introduce the horizontal line  \n\n   \\[\n   H_b:\\; y=b .\n   \\]\n\n   These lines have slope \\(0\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.  \n   Their number is \\(n-k\\).\n\n5. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   *If a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to one of the omitted rows \\(\\{b_1,\\dots ,b_k\\}\\),* then by definition it lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_i\\) that was drawn through the chosen point of that row; the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\) contains all points of the form \\((x, i x + (b_i-i a_i))\\), and in particular \\((a,b_i)\\) satisfies it because the constant term was chosen so that \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and the slope is fixed.  Direct substitution shows that \\((a,b_i)\\) indeed satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n   *If a point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to a row not omitted,* then \\(b\\) is one of the indices used for a horizontal line, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(H_b\\).\n\n   Hence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered by at least one line of the family.\n\n6. **Counting lines.**  \n   The family consists of the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) and the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(H_b\\); altogether exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) (and \\(n\\ge4\\)) we have exhibited a configuration containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The exceptional case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains three points.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The three horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3\\) (the last one is unnecessary but allowed) are all non‑sunny and cover the set.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the two horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\;y=2\\) and the sunny line \\(\\ell: y=2x\\), which passes through \\((1,2)\\).  The point \\((2,1)\\) lies on \\(y=1\\) and \\((1,1)\\) on \\(y=1\\) as well, so all three points are covered.\n\n* **\\(k=2\\) is impossible.**  \n  Suppose a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of three distinct lines covers \\(T_3\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.  \n  Any line that contains two of the three points must have slope \\(0\\) (through \\((1,1),(2,1)\\)), \\(\\infty\\) (through \\((1,1),(1,2)\\)) or \\(-1\\) (through \\((1,2),(2,1)\\)).  \n  Therefore a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(T_3\\).  \n  With only two sunny lines we can cover at most two points, leaving at least one point that must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  \n  The remaining point also lies on a line of forbidden slope, so a distinct non‑sunny line would be required, contradicting the assumption that exactly two lines are sunny.  \n  Hence no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Take three sunny lines, each passing through a different point of \\(T_3\\); for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=2x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+2 .\n  \\]\n\n  Their slopes are \\(1,2,-\\tfrac12\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the lines are distinct and together cover the three points.  Thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\nConsequently, for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the constructions of Sections 1–4 we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\ &&k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\ge4:\\ &&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the non‑negative integers for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
